{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/figure-fields-2/reader-packet-1-part3.json'","aggregated_output":"{\n \"articles\": [\n  {\n   \"article\": 1,\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia Gains as Apple Pulls AI Off the Meter\",\n   \"publisher\": \"gurufocus.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-22\",\n   \"text\": \"Nvidia ( NASDAQ:NVDA ), the AI-accelerator and data-center platform leader, faced a fresh edge-computing test as Apple ( NASDAQ:AAPL ) pushed its new Macs as machines capable of running heavyweight AI privately instead of constantly calling the cloud. Nvidia shares gained approximately 0.7% to $229.08, suggesting investors were hardly treating Apple's move as an immediate threat to the GPU giant's core data-center engine.\\nWarning! GuruFocus has detected 3 Warning Sign with MSFT.\\nApple showed four Mac Studios working together to run a trillion-parameter model, using shared memory across the machines to tackle workloads once associated almost entirely with expensive server infrastructure. That is an eye-catching demonstration, but the scale gap remains enormous: Nvidia's latest quarter generated $89 billion of data-center revenue out of $96.2 billion overall, meaning roughly 92.5% of sales came from the infrastructure businesses powering large-scale AI. The GuruFocus picture reinforces that underlying strength, with Nvidia carrying a GF Score of 95/100 and particularly strong readings for profitability, growth and financial strength, while GF Value remains the noticeably weaker part of the profile.\\nThe bigger question is not whether a handful of Macs can run a giant model. They clearly can. The question is how much inference, coding and repetitive enterprise AI work permanently shifts from cloud infrastructure onto local machines. Apple could chip away at some private and predictable workloads, especially where data security and recurring token costs matter. But hyperscale training, massive networking demands and unpredictable peak workloads still play directly into Nvidia's territory. For Nvidia investors, the real risk starts only if local AI becomes a structural substitute for cloud inference rather than another layer of the computing stack.\",\n   \"named_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"figure\": 1,\n     \"text\": \"$89 billion of data-center revenue in the latest quarter\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"open_questions\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n     \"question\": \"What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"article\": 2,\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia stock is approaching its cheapest valuation in a decade despite booming profits\",\n   \"publisher\": \"qz.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-22\",\n   \"text\": \"Nvidia stock is trading at less than 17 times forward earnings, half the multiple it commanded in 2025, even as revenue and net income are expected to surge\\nNvidia $NVDA stock is trading near its lowest valuation in more than a decade, according to Bloomberg , even as the chipmaker's revenue and net income are each expected to grow by roughly 90% or more in its current fiscal year.\\nAt under 17 times forward earnings, the stock's multiple has been cut in half compared with 2025 — a period when Nvidia 's expansion was actually slower — and has fallen sharply from above 25 times expected profits just this past May, according to Bloomberg.\\n\\\"The stock has de-rated pretty significantly, which suggests a healthy dose of skepticism that the company's current earnings power is sustainable,\\\" Eli Horton, a TCW senior portfolio manager overseeing thematic and durable growth equities, told Bloomberg. \\\"The stock's performance is surprising, given the backdrop of incredible fundamentals, but it tells you the market is expecting less than what the consensus is currently estimating.\\\"\\nOne drag on the stock's valuation is a squeeze on profitability. Nvidia 's gross margin came in at 75% last quarter but analyst estimates compiled by Bloomberg show it sliding under 72% by the fourth quarter, with a recovery expected thereafter. Rising costs for key components such as memory chips are a central factor.\\nCompetition is another concern. David Russell, global head of market strategy at TradeStation, pointed to the growing threat of customers building their own silicon, noting that Meta $META Platforms and Alphabet $GOOGL are among the major Nvidia buyers that have moved to design AI chips internally — a shift he believes will gradually erode Nvidia 's pricing power and market share. \\\"Multiples expand when companies are well positioned with potential to get better, and Nvidia doesn't offer that,\\\" Russell said.\\nNvidia stock has gained 22% so far in 2026, putting it second among the Magnificent Seven behind Apple $AAPL 's 25% advance — yet that performance looks modest against the broader chip sector. The Philadelphia Stock Exchange Semiconductor Index has surged nearly 76%, with Intel $INTC and Advanced Micro Devices both more than tripling; Nvidia sits near the bottom of the index's leaderboard, even as the index itself carries a valuation of 20 times forward earnings.\\nNvidia Chief Executive Officer Jensen Huang has pushed back on the market's assessment, calling the company \\\"the world's first and only growth value stock\\\" and describing Nvidia as \\\"incredibly misunderstood\\\" at a Goldman Sachs $GS technology conference earlier this month.\\nThe valuation discount follows a stretch of strong results. Nvidia stock climbed roughly 7% after its second-quarter earnings , when revenue more than doubled and the company projected 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028 — well above the 45% analysts had expected at the time.\\nHorton said the current multiple looks favorable given that neither a pullback in AI infrastructure spending nor a major regulatory intervention appears imminent. \\\"This seems like a very favorable multiple to have as an entry point,\\\" he said.\",\n   \"named_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"figure\": 1,\n     \"text\": \"the 45% revenue growth analysts had expected for fiscal 2028\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"figure\": 2,\n     \"text\": \"the 70% revenue growth projected for fiscal 2028\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"figure\": 3,\n     \"text\": \"gross margin sliding under 72% by the fourth quarter\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"open_questions\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n     \"question\": \"What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n     \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"article\": 3,\n   \"title\": \"Will Nvidia be allowed to sell more chips to China? This former Trump economic adviser has a hot take.\",\n   \"publisher\": \"finance.yahoo.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-23\",\n   \"text\": \"Will Nvidia be allowed to sell more chips to China? This former Trump economic adviser has a hot take.\\nWhen Nvidia ( NVDA ) CEO Jensen Huang arrives at the state dinner on Thursday between President Trump and Chinese President Xi Jinping, you better believe he'll be hoping to get a sense of when — and to what extent — his business may resume in China.\\nBoth sides coming to a deal on this front won't be easy, given the politics still at play.\\n\\\"My personal opinion is that we need to have as much competitive advantage here in the United States as we can, because we've got competitive disadvantages against China,\\\" said Gary Cohn, IBM ( IBM ) vice chair and former economic adviser to President Trump in his first term, on Yahoo Finance's Sozzi Unleashed . \\\"You know, they don't have environmental issues. They don't have a cost of capital. They can build data centers wherever they want. The citizens can't stand up and say not in my backyard.\\\"\\nCohn added, \\\"To the extent that the Chinese have all these other significant advantages, the one advantage we have is we've got the chips, and we've got the chip manufacturing here. I'd like to keep that advantage as long as we can. \\\"\\nThe Trump administration has allowed Nvidia to ship its H200 AI chips to approved customers in China. The problem for Nvidia: The H200 is already an older-generation product, trailing Nvidia's current Blackwell chips in terms of performance.\\nIn other words, Nvidia is likely leaving billions in sales on the table by being unable to get Blackwell chips into China to feed the country's AI startup scene.\\nChinese authorities, however, have so far restricted shipments of Nvidia's H200 chips.\\nWhile Nvidia hasn't factored any sales from China into its outlook for the year, the stock is still up 21% year to date, powered by the AI boom here in the US.\",\n   \"named_figures\": [],\n   \"open_questions\": null\n  },\n  {\n   \"article\": 4,\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia Stock Could Surge by 2028: Here's How Beginners Can Get In Now According to Motley Fool's Price Estimates\",\n   \"publisher\": \"fool.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-23\",\n   \"text\": \"Even if you're a total novice to the stock market, you've probably heard of Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) .\\nNvidia is now the most valuable company in the world, worth more than $5 trillion. Its chips have formed the backbone of the AI revolution, and the top AI labs and cloud computing platforms rely on them to make AI applications run.\\nIf you were fortunate enough to buy Nvidia a decade ago and hold it, you would have made a huge return as the stock is up more than 14,000% during that time. That means a $1,000 investment in Nvidia would now be worth more than $14,000.\\nThe stock won't do that again over the next decade, but it still looks poised to soar by 2028. Here's how I see it.\\nThere's a lot of jargon in investing, but one of the most important concepts that beginning investors can learn is the price-to-earnings ratio , or P/E ratio. This is a company's price per share divided by its earnings per share.\\nNewbie investors often think that a stock's share price determines whether it is cheap or not. For instance, a $10 stock would be cheap and a $500 stock would be expensive, but the share price alone isn't really meaningful.\\nOwning a share of a stock is a claim on its earnings, so what really matters is how much earnings you get relative to the price of the stock. That's the P/E ratio. The lower it is, the cheaper the stock.\\nNvidia now trades at a P/E of 29, which is slightly higher than the S&P 500's P/E ratio of 25. The S&P 500 , an index that holds 500 of the biggest U.S. stocks, is generally seen as the market average. According to the numbers above, Nvidia is a bit more expensive than the S&P 500. However, the P/E ratio is backward-looking, and stocks are priced based on future earnings or cash flows.\\nBecause of its central position in the AI boom, Nvidia is growing much faster than the S&P 500. Wall Street expects the company to grow revenue by 91% this year and 66% next year. For fiscal 2028, which ends in January of that year, Nvidia is expected to have earnings per share of $15.68.\\nBased on that forecast, the stock trades at 14.6x next year's earnings. or a forward P/E of 14.6. If Nvidia maintains its current P/E ratio and hits that EPS forecast, the stock would double in less than a year and a half.\\nIf you're new to investing and you want to buy Nvidia stock , you'll first need a brokerage account if you don't already have one. You can open one up with a platform like Robinhood , Interactive Brokers , E*TRADE from Morgan Stanley , or Charles Schwab . There are a lot of options for brokers.\\nNvidia is currently trading around $228 a share, so you'll need that much to buy one share. However, many brokerages now offer fractional share purchasing , meaning you can buy a fraction of a share of Nvidia, allowing you to get ownership for less than the price of a share. Once you have an account, you'll need to search for the stock using the ticker, NVDA, and then choose an order type. Using a market order will execute the trade immediately and is the easiest way to buy a heavily traded stock like Nvidia. If you want to set a maximum price you're willing to pay, you can use a limit order.\\nKeep in mind that while Nvidia's future looks promising and the numbers favor continued gains in the stock, there is always risk in stock investing. There is no guarantee that the stock will go up between now and 2028. With any investment, it's a good idea to keep a long-term view and continue to follow the stock. Don't just buy and forget. If things change, you may want to sell.\\nStill, I think the odds are in your favor. As long as the AI boom continues over the next two years, Nvidia looks well-positioned to be a winner.\\nCharles Schwab is an advertising partner of Motley Fool Money. Jeremy Bowman has positions in Nvidia. The Motley Fool has positions in and recommends Interactive Brokers Group and Nvidia. The Motley Fool recommends Charles Schwab and recommends the following options: long January 2027 $43.75 calls on Interactive Brokers Group, short January 2027 $46.25 calls on Interactive Brokers Group, and short September 2026 $95 calls on Charles Schwab. The Motley Fool has a disclosure policy .\\nBy Jeremy Bowman – Sep 23, 2026 at 12:00AM EST\",\n   \"named_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"figure\": 1,\n     \"text\": \"earnings per share of $15.68 expected for fiscal 2028\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"open_questions\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n     \"question\": \"What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n     \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n     \"question\": \"Will Nvidia be permitted to sell its current Blackwell-generation AI chips into China — through U.S. export approval and Chinese acceptance of shipments — by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n     \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end, used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3 (item gives no date of its own).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n     \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, which the company says has entered full production, reach volume customer shipments (first revenue recognition) by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n     \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 (FY2027), checked at NVIDIA's quarterly results and FY2027 Form 10-K. The item gives no shipment date; the current fiscal year-end following the September 2026 report that Vera Rubin entered full production is used as the check date.\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n     \"question\": \"How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2026?\",\n     \"period\": \"Calendar year 2026 (the item's 'this year' in a 23 September 2026 article), checked at the hyperscalers' Q4/full-year 2026 earnings and capex disclosures in early 2027.\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"article\": 5,\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia’s Last Quarter Was Nearly Perfect. One Number in the Fine Print Wasn’t.\",\n   \"publisher\": \"fool.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-23\",\n   \"text\": \"Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) recently reported $96.2 billion in revenue for the fiscal second quarter, which was 106% more than in the same period last year. The company also produced a 75% gross margin, and management told investors to expect roughly 70% revenue growth in the next fiscal year.\\nBy most numbers, Nvidia reported a stellar quarter. However, a closer look at the company's balance sheet shows that Nvidia's total assets jumped by more than 50% over the past six months. Here's why that matters, and what I'll be watching closely over the next few quarters of Nvidia's AI-driven growth.\\nNvidia's spectacular quarter, with one caveat\\nIt's difficult to overstate how strong Nvidia's fiscal second quarter was. Revenue and earnings both handily beat expectations, and Nvidia guided to a jaw-dropping $108 billion in revenue for the current quarter, representing 17% sequential growth.\\nAs mentioned earlier, Nvidia's total assets grew rapidly. A big part of that is the company's receivables rising from $38.5 billion in January to $63.1 billion at the end of July.\\nOf course, some growth in receivables is to be expected, given Nvidia's spectacular revenue growth. After all, if you sell more products, you can expect more unpaid invoices at any given time. But one number to note is \\\"days sales outstanding,\\\" or DSO. This is the measure of how long, on average, it takes a company to collect after making a sale. Nvidia's DSO went from 45 to 60 days in one quarter. Customers are taking longer to pay.\\nTo be fair, Nvidia told us why. The company extended payment terms on large orders from certain customers.\\nOn one hand, Nvidia offering of flexible terms to its strongest customers helps them lock in multi-year deals. On the other hand, Nvidia is increasingly helping finance its customers' orders, which raises the question of whether demand appears higher in the revenue numbers than it otherwise would.\\nThis is just the latest in a string of \\\"circular\\\" financial moves. For example, Nvidia agreed to guarantee up to $105 billion of data center lease obligations for OpenAI. It has formed partnerships to raise over $500 billion of outside capital for AI infrastructure. In simple terms, Nvidia is actively helping customers pay for its products.\\nThis is especially worth watching for Nvidia stock investors, given the recent push to slow down the pace of AI development. While a call to slow AI development isn't the same thing as a cut to infrastructure budgets, the DSO number, as well as receivables and inventories, are certainly worth keeping an eye on over the next few quarters.\\nIf the DSO number stabilizes, and receivables and inventories rise in line with Nvidia's revenue, it's a very good sign. On the other hand, if these numbers continue to grow faster than the top line, it could indicate a spending slowdown.\",\n   \"named_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"figure\": 1,\n     \"text\": \"$96.2 billion in revenue for the fiscal second quarter\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"figure\": 2,\n     \"text\": \"roughly 70% revenue growth in the next fiscal year\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"open_questions\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n     \"question\": \"What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n     \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n     \"question\": \"Will Nvidia be permitted to sell its current Blackwell-generation AI chips into China — through U.S. export approval and Chinese acceptance of shipments — by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n     \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end, used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3 (item gives no date of its own).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n     \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, which the company says has entered full production, reach volume customer shipments (first revenue recognition) by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n     \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 (FY2027), checked at NVIDIA's quarterly results and FY2027 Form 10-K. The item gives no shipment date; the current fiscal year-end following the September 2026 report that Vera Rubin entered full production is used as the check date.\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n     \"question\": \"How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2026?\",\n     \"period\": \"Calendar year 2026 (the item's 'this year' in a 23 September 2026 article), checked at the hyperscalers' Q4/full-year 2026 earnings and capex disclosures in early 2027.\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"article\": 6,\n   \"title\": \"I'm Buying More Nvidia Stock Right Now. Here's Why.\",\n   \"publisher\": \"fool.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-24\",\n   \"text\": \"Nvidia (NASDAQ: NVDA) has grown into the world's largest company, with a market cap of more than $5 trillion. Its graphics processing units (GPUs) are the gold standard for training and running high-level computing and artificial intelligence programs.\\nSome people may, however, look at Nvidia's stock price and think that the shine is coming off Jensen Huang's company. Shares are up 21% so far this year, which is fine, but that's not even close to the 239% gain in 2023, the 171% jump in 2024, or even last year's 39% increase. Meanwhile, stocks like Sandisk , Micron , and Intel are all up more than 200% this year and have become Wall Street darlings.\\nAre Nvidia's best days in the rearview mirror? Absolutely not. While the chipmaker may not replicate its massive 2023 gain, Nvidia remains an incredibly profitable company whose growth story is far from over. In fact, I think Nvidia is going to be putting up some wild numbers in the next two or three years, and that's why I'll be buying more Nvidia shares .\\nNvidia is still growing fast, despite its size\\nWhat stands out in Nvidia's most recent earnings report is that the company is still growing at a triple-digit rate. Revenue in the second quarter of fiscal 2027 (ending July 26, 2026) was $96.2 billion, up 106% from a year ago. Of that, $89 billion, or roughly 92.5% of the company's total revenue, came from its data center segment.\\nNet income was $59.68 billion, up 126% from a year ago, and earnings per share of $2.46 were up 128% from last year. Nvidia is very profitable -- its operating expenses totaled only $8.4 billion for the quarter, and its gross margin was an impressive 75%.\\n\\\"We're the only company in the world that creates and builds, offers an entire AI factory platform, a full-stack system. And customers can still mix and match,\\\" CEO Jensen Huang said. \\\"However, most companies just don't have the skills to do that or desire to do that. And so, there's an entire part of the market that we experience growth. There's sovereign AI, there're regional AIs, there're neoclouds, there're AI start-ups, there're enterprises, where we're seeing -- which represents about half of our business, and that's growing 100% a year.\\\"\\nNvidia's new product line is just getting started\\nNvidia's Hopper and Blackwell architectures have been enormously popular among hyperscalers and neoclouds building computing capacity to meet the growing demand for AI. But the company's Vera Rubin architecture, which uses NVLink chip-to-chip interconnects so its Rubin GPUs and Vera central processing units (CPUs) can share memory and improve efficiency, generates even more revenue. CFO Colette Kress said the Vera Rubin -- now in full production -- and full-stack AI platform has expanded the company's total addressable market from a range of $18 billion to $25 billion per gigawatt from Blackwell to $40 billion from Vera Rubin.\\nThat's one of the reasons why Huang has been so confident in his prediction -- which he made last year and recently reiterated -- that AI infrastructure spending would reach $3 trillion to $4 trillion by 2030. And speaking to reporters last week while in Scotland, Huang suggested that Nvidia would double its chip sales in the coming year.\\nIt's hard to get a group of people to agree on anything -- that's just human nature -- but Wall Street experts are nearly unanimous on Nvidia stock. Analysts at Rosenblatt have a \\\"Buy\\\" recommendation and a $390 price target, representing potential upside of 73%. Bernstein's analysts have a $400 price target and an \\\"Outperform\\\" rating, and Raymond James analysts have a whopping $515 price target and a \\\"Strong Buy\\\" rating.\\nNvidia is expected to report another strong quarter when it next reports earnings (likely in late November), with the consensus revenue estimate of $109.0 billion, up 91.2% from the previous year. And more of the same is expected for next year, following the Vera Rubin ramp-up.\",\n   \"named_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"figure\": 1,\n     \"text\": \"the consensus revenue estimate of $109.0 billion for the next report\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"figure\": 2,\n     \"text\": \"revenue of $96.2 billion in the second quarter of fiscal 2027\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"open_questions\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n     \"question\": \"What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n     \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n     \"question\": \"Will Nvidia be permitted to sell its current Blackwell-generation AI chips into China — through U.S. export approval and Chinese acceptance of shipments — by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n     \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end, used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3 (item gives no date of its own).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n     \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, which the company says has entered full production, reach volume customer shipments (first revenue recognition) by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n     \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 (FY2027), checked at NVIDIA's quarterly results and FY2027 Form 10-K. The item gives no shipment date; the current fiscal year-end following the September 2026 report that Vera Rubin entered full production is used as the check date.\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n     \"question\": \"How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2026?\",\n     \"period\": \"Calendar year 2026 (the item's 'this year' in a 23 September 2026 article), checked at the hyperscalers' Q4/full-year 2026 earnings and capex disclosures in early 2027.\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"article\": 7,\n   \"title\": \"NVIDIA Stock Looks Expensive Until You Price The Vera Rubin Ramp\",\n   \"publisher\": \"trefis.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-24\",\n   \"text\": \"NVIDIA Stock Looks Expensive Until You Price The Vera Rubin Ramp\\nNVIDIA (NVDA) stock trades near $225, about 28.3 times its adjusted earnings over the past twelve months. Adjusted here means normalized net income with stock-based compensation added back, the same broad basis analysts use in their forecasts. For a stock near its 52-week high, that looks steep. But the multiple leaves out the growth analysts already expect, and that growth depends on how much NVIDIA can supply.\\nNVIDIA Looks Ordinary Once The Forecast Earnings Arrive\\nOn the earnings analysts expect for fiscal 2027, the same price is about 24.1 times. On what they expect for fiscal 2028, it is about 14.5 times. The price is the same. The earnings analysts expect it to buy are not.\\nNVIDIA expects Vera Rubin, its newest platform, to be the fastest product ramp in the company’s history. Management puts NVIDIA’s revenue opportunity at about $40 billion per gigawatt of capacity with Vera Rubin, against $25 billion with Blackwell. Production shipments began in August, with purchase orders already in from every major hyperscaler, AI cloud, and system OEM.\\nSo What Stands Between NVIDIA And Those Earnings?\\nFor fiscal 2028, management has guided about 70% revenue growth, the first time it has guided a full year ahead. That guide does not depend on finding more demand. The CEO says demand is much greater than 70%, and that supply is what holds the guide there. The CFO expects supply to stay a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028 and calls memory pricing extreme.\\nSome of that demand also leans on NVIDIA’s own balance sheet. NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in frontier AI labs. Management concedes some will call this circular financing and says the compute can be redeployed to other customers.\\nAnalysts also disagree on what that growth earns. The 33 estimates of fiscal 2028 earnings run from $12.62 to $17.49 a share, a wide band for the year the forward multiple is priced on.\\nAnd NVIDIA’s Gross Margin Is Guided Lower As Memory Costs Climb\\nConsensus has earnings and revenue growing at a similar pace between fiscal 2027 and fiscal 2028, so analysts assume profit margins hold roughly steady. Gross margin is guided lower. Memory prices have risen more than management expected, and the guide has that margin settling in the 72% to 73% range in fiscal 2028, helped by price increases NVIDIA has already executed.\\nFor the forecast to hold, that lost gross margin has to be made up further down the income statement. That is the second condition behind the fiscal 2028 multiple, alongside supply.\\nSo Is NVIDIA Cheap, Or Only If The Chips Get Built?\\nOnly if the chips get built, which means the supply arrives and the margins hold. The trailing multiple cannot tell you either. How much would you stake on a year that has not started?\\nChecking that for every stock you own is a job most people have no time for. Since its inception, our rule-based Trefis High Quality Portfolio has outperformed its benchmark, a blend of three major indices.\\nOr hunt for these yourself: our Forward Valuation Discount screen ranks stocks priced furthest below their expected earnings. It shows where to look, and the forecast still has to come true.\",\n   \"named_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"figure\": 1,\n     \"text\": \"about 70% revenue growth guided for fiscal 2028\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"figure\": 2,\n     \"text\": \"the margin settling in the 72% to 73% range in fiscal 2028\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"open_questions\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n     \"question\": \"What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n     \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n     \"question\": \"Will Nvidia be permitted to sell its current Blackwell-generation AI chips into China — through U.S. export approval and Chinese acceptance of shipments — by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n     \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end, used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3 (item gives no date of its own).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n     \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, which the company says has entered full production, reach volume customer shipments (first revenue recognition) by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n     \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 (FY2027), checked at NVIDIA's quarterly results and FY2027 Form 10-K. The item gives no shipment date; the current fiscal year-end following the September 2026 report that Vera Rubin entered full production is used as the check date.\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n     \"question\": \"How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2026?\",\n     \"period\": \"Calendar year 2026 (the item's 'this year' in a 23 September 2026 article), checked at the hyperscalers' Q4/full-year 2026 earnings and capex disclosures in early 2027.\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"article\": 8,\n   \"title\": \"Credo vs. Nvidia: Which AI Chip Stock Is a Better Buy in 2026?\",\n   \"publisher\": \"fool.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-25\",\n   \"text\": \"The artificial intelligence boom has transformed the semiconductor landscape. Deciding between a high-growth connectivity specialist like Credo Technology Group ( CRDO +5.75% ) and the industry giant Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) requires a look at their specific niches.\\nBoth companies are central to modern data centers, but they solve different problems. Credo provides the high-speed wiring and connectivity chips that keep data moving, while Nvidia supplies the massive processing power AI models require. Comparing these two reveals how different players profit from the same infrastructure trend.\\nCredo designs high-speed copper and optical interconnect solutions essential for modern AI-driven infrastructure. The company operates as a niche provider of high-speed connectivity solutions among semiconductor stocks serving AI data centers. Credo relies on a limited number of customers for a substantial portion of its revenue, with sales to its top 10 customers accounting for approximately 90% of total revenue in fiscal 2026. This level of customer concentration adds risk to the business. The company also maintains a partnership with Oracle ( ORCL -0.13% ) to develop ZeroFlap optics to improve reliability in AI data centers.\\nFinancial performance has accelerated significantly recently. In fiscal 2026, which ended in May 2026, revenue reached nearly $1.3 billion, up roughly 205.7% from the previous year. This growth helped the company achieve a net income of approximately $472.3 million. The net margin for the period was approximately 35.4%, a substantial improvement from the 11.9% net margin reported in the prior fiscal year.\\nThe balance sheet remains highly liquid. As of its May 2026 balance sheet, the current ratio (a measure of short-term debt-paying ability) is nearly 10.2x. The debt-to-equity ratio (total debt compared to equity) is 0.0x, indicating no debt relative to equity. Free cash flow (cash from operations minus capital expenditures) was nearly $407.0 million. Stock-based compensation represented roughly 39.3% of operating cash flow, which inflates reported cash generation because SBC is a non-cash expense added back in the cash flow statement.\\nNvidia is the dominant provider of GPU-accelerated computing platforms used in gaming, professional visualization, and data centers. The company sells its products to massive cloud service providers and AI model makers globally. Nvidia relies on a concentrated customer base, with sales to two direct customers representing 22% and 14% of total revenue in fiscal 2026, respectively. This level of customer concentration adds a layer of risk to the business. The company also recently agreed to acquire Hugging Face for approximately $12.9 billion to expand its software capabilities.\\nRevenue growth remains a primary driver for the business. In fiscal 2026, which ended in January 2026, revenue reached nearly $215.9 billion, up roughly 65.5% year over year. This scale produced net income of approximately $120.1 billion. The net margin remained remarkably high at approximately 55.6%, consistent with the 55.8% net margin achieved in the previous fiscal year.\\nStrong profitability supports a robust capital structure. As of its January 2026 balance sheet, the current ratio is approximately 3.9x. The debt-to-equity ratio (which compares total debt to shareholder equity) is under 0.1x, meaning the company carries very little debt. Free cash flow (cash from operations minus capital expenditures) reached nearly $96.7 billion for the fiscal year. This massive cash generation provides significant flexibility for research, development, and strategic acquisitions.\\nCredo faces intense competition from larger, well-established semiconductor companies and potential new entrants in the data infrastructure market. The company depends heavily on a small number of customers, making it vulnerable to losses or shifts in purchasing patterns. Its business model relies on third-party manufacturing partners, exposing the company to supply chain disruptions and geopolitical risks regarding operations in Taiwan and mainland China. Furthermore, evolving international trade controls and export restrictions could restrict its ability to serve key markets.\\nNvidia faces significant risks from global geopolitical tensions and export control regulations, particularly for high-performance AI chips. These restrictions have impacted sales to China and could continue to limit market access. The company faces intense competition from other hardware suppliers and internal development efforts by customers like Microsoft ( MSFT +1.22% ) , Amazon ( AMZN +0.51% ) , and Alphabet ( GOOGL -0.61% ) ( GOOG -0.77% ) . Nvidia also manages various legal challenges, including inquiries from competition regulators worldwide regarding its market practices.\\nNvidia trades at a lower forward P/E based on future earnings estimates, while both carry a high P/S ratio compared to historical averages.\\nI'd buy Nvidia, and it isn't close. Here's why:\\nCredo's growth is not the issue. Revenue in its latest quarter more than doubled from a year earlier, and management guided the current quarter higher still. The stock fell anyway because gross margin guidance came in flat, and shares now sit well below their 52-week high. That's the problem with a company that gets about 90% of its revenue from 10 customers. Any hint of slower spending from one of them moves the stock, and the valuation leaves no room for a miss.\\nNvidia is growing nearly as fast at a scale that makes Credo look like a rounding error. Revenue in its latest quarter also more than doubled, with almost all of it coming from data center customers, and management's outlook for the current quarter assumes no data center compute sales to China at all. Nvidia has its own concentration risk, with two customers making up more than a third of sales, and the Hugging Face deal shows it's willing to spend to stay ahead.\\nFor a long-term investor, Nvidia offers the same AI infrastructure exposure at a lower price per dollar of expected earnings, with far less dependence on any single buyer. Credo belongs in a portfolio only as a small position you can afford to watch swing.\\nCredo more than tripled revenue last year but leans on 10 customers for 90% of sales, while Nvidia is doubling revenue at a scale nobody else in AI hardware can match.\\nBy Mike Schwenk – Updated Sep 25, 2026 at 3:00PM EST\\nCredo makes the high-speed connectivity chips and cables that move data between servers inside AI data centers, and revenue more than tripled in fiscal 2026.\\nNvidia's data center business brought in $89 billion in its latest quarter alone, and management guided the next quarter to $108 billion, excluding any China compute sales.\\nBoth companies depend on a small group of customers, but Nvidia's scale, margins, and lower valuation make it the stronger buy today.\",\n   \"named_figures\": [],\n   \"open_questions\": null\n  }\n ],\n \"answers\": [\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B21\",\n   \"article\": 1,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-1\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's latest reported quarter generated $89 billion of data-center revenue out of $96.2 billion in total revenue, with GuruFocus calculating that roughly 92.5% of sales came from the infrastructure businesses powering large-scale AI. The item does not specify which fiscal quarter this is.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Nvidia's latest quarter generated $89 billion of data-center revenue out of $96.2 billion overall, meaning roughly 92.5% of sales came from the infrastructure businesses powering large-scale AI.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"data-center revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"latest quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$89 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"data-center / AI infrastructure businesses\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"total revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"latest quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$96.2 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B22\",\n   \"article\": 5,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e1\",\n     \"summary\": \"Nvidia reported $96.2 billion of revenue for its fiscal second quarter, 106% more than in the same period a year earlier, at a 75% gross margin; the article says revenue and earnings handily beat expectations.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) recently reported $96.2 billion in revenue for the fiscal second quarter, which was 106% more than in the same period last year. The company also produced a 75% gross margin\",\n      \"Revenue and earnings both handily beat expectations\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Nvidia\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$96.2 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue growth\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter versus same period last year\",\n       \"value\": \"106%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"growth over the same period last year\",\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"75%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e2\",\n     \"summary\": \"Nvidia guided investors to $108 billion of revenue for the current quarter, which it said represents 17% sequential growth.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Nvidia guided to a jaw-dropping $108 billion in revenue for the current quarter, representing 17% sequential growth.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Nvidia\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"current quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$108 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"sequential revenue growth\",\n       \"period\": \"current quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"17%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"sequential growth\",\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e3\",\n     \"summary\": \"Nvidia management told investors to expect roughly 70% revenue growth in the next fiscal year.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"management told investors to expect roughly 70% revenue growth in the next fiscal year.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Nvidia\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue growth guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"next fiscal year\",\n       \"value\": \"70%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"roughly\",\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e5\",\n     \"summary\": \"Nvidia's receivables rose from $38.5 billion in January to $63.1 billion at the end of July, as total assets jumped by more than 50% over the past six months, and days sales outstanding rose from 45 to 60 days in one quarter; Nvidia said it extended payment terms on large orders from certain customers.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"A big part of that is the company's receivables rising from $38.5 billion in January to $63.1 billion at the end of July.\",\n      \"Nvidia's DSO went from 45 to 60 days in one quarter. Customers are taking longer to pay.\",\n      \"To be fair, Nvidia told us why. The company extended payment terms on large orders from certain customers.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Nvidia\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"accounts receivable\",\n       \"period\": \"January\",\n       \"value\": \"$38.5 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"accounts receivable\",\n       \"period\": \"end of July\",\n       \"value\": \"$63.1 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"days sales outstanding\",\n       \"period\": \"one quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"60 days\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"up from 45 days\",\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-1\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"Shows NVIDIA financing customers' purchases through extended payment terms on large orders, alongside the formal guarantees the thesis tracks; the receivables jump and DSO rise of 45 to 60 days indicate the scale of that customer credit exposure, but the item gives no guarantee amounts.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B23\",\n   \"article\": 4,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-1\",\n     \"summary\": \"Motley Fool contributor Jeremy Bowman reported that Wall Street expects Nvidia to grow revenue by 91% this year and 66% next year, and that Nvidia is expected to earn $15.68 per share in fiscal 2028, which the article says ends in January of that year. These are third-party/consensus forecasts, not company guidance; the article gives no period year for the two revenue growth rates beyond 'this year' and 'next year'.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Wall Street expects the company to grow revenue by 91% this year and 66% next year. For fiscal 2028, which ends in January of that year, Nvidia is expected to have earnings per share of $15.68.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"consensus\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue growth\",\n       \"period\": \"this year\",\n       \"value\": \"91%\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"company revenue growth, attributed to Wall Street expectations\",\n       \"party\": \"consensus\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue growth\",\n       \"period\": \"next year\",\n       \"value\": \"66%\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"company revenue growth, attributed to Wall Street expectations\",\n       \"party\": \"consensus\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"earnings per share\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028, which ends in January of that year\",\n       \"value\": \"$15.68\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"expected EPS, source not named beyond 'expected'\",\n       \"party\": \"consensus\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B24\",\n   \"article\": 2,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e1\",\n     \"summary\": \"Nvidia's revenue and net income are each expected to grow by roughly 90% or more in its current fiscal year, according to estimates reported by Bloomberg, even as the stock trades near its lowest valuation in more than a decade.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Nvidia $NVDA stock is trading near its lowest valuation in more than a decade, according to Bloomberg , even as the chipmaker's revenue and net income are each expected to grow by roughly 90% or more in its current fiscal year.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Bloomberg (compiled estimates)\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue growth\",\n       \"period\": \"Current fiscal year\",\n       \"value\": \"roughly 90% or more\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"Company-level revenue growth expected, per Bloomberg\",\n       \"party\": \"Bloomberg (compiled estimates)\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Net income growth\",\n       \"period\": \"Current fiscal year\",\n       \"value\": \"roughly 90% or more\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"Company-level net income growth expected, per Bloomberg\",\n       \"party\": \"Bloomberg (compiled estimates)\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e2\",\n     \"summary\": \"Nvidia's gross margin came in at 75% in the last reported quarter, and analyst estimates compiled by Bloomberg show it sliding under 72% by the fourth quarter, with a recovery expected thereafter; rising costs for key components such as memory chips are cited as a central factor.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"One drag on the stock's valuation is a squeeze on profitability. Nvidia 's gross margin came in at 75% last quarter but analyst estimates compiled by Bloomberg show it sliding under 72% by the fourth quarter, with a recovery expected thereafter. Rising costs for key components such as memory chips are a central factor.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Nvidia (reported gross margin); Bloomberg-compiled analyst estimates\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"Last quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"75%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"Company-level gross margin, as reported\",\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia (reported gross margin); Bloomberg-compiled analyst estimates\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"Fourth quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"under 72%\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"Analyst estimates compiled by Bloomberg\",\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia (reported gross margin); Bloomberg-compiled analyst estimates\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e3\",\n     \"summary\": \"Nvidia reported second-quarter revenue that more than doubled year over year and projected 70% revenue growth for fiscal 2028, well above the 45% analysts had expected at the time; the stock rose roughly 7% after the earnings report.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"The valuation discount follows a stretch of strong results. Nvidia stock climbed roughly 7% after its second-quarter earnings , when revenue more than doubled and the company projected 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028 — well above the 45% analysts had expected at the time.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Nvidia (results and guidance); analysts (prior expectation)\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue growth\",\n       \"period\": \"Second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"more than doubled\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"Year-over-year company revenue growth, as reported\",\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia (results and guidance); analysts (prior expectation)\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue growth guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"Fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"70%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"Company projection, per the article\",\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia (results and guidance); analysts (prior expectation)\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue growth expectation\",\n       \"period\": \"Fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"45%\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"What analysts had expected at the time, per the article\",\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia (results and guidance); analysts (prior expectation)\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B25\",\n   \"article\": 6,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e1\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA reported second-quarter fiscal 2027 (quarter ended 26 July 2026) revenue of $96.2 billion, up 106% from a year earlier, of which $89 billion — roughly 92.5% of total revenue — came from the data center segment. Net income was $59.68 billion, up 126%, and earnings per share were $2.46, up 128%. Operating expenses totaled $8.4 billion for the quarter and gross margin was 75%.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Revenue in the second quarter of fiscal 2027 (ending July 26, 2026) was $96.2 billion, up 106% from a year ago. Of that, $89 billion, or roughly 92.5% of the company's total revenue, came from its data center segment.\",\n      \"Net income was $59.68 billion, up 126% from a year ago, and earnings per share of $2.46 were up 128% from last year. Nvidia is very profitable -- its operating expenses totaled only $8.4 billion for the quarter, and its gross margin was an impressive 75%.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"second quarter of fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$96.2 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue growth year over year\",\n       \"period\": \"second quarter of fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"106%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"Data center segment revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"second quarter of fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$89 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"data center segment; roughly 92.5% of total revenue\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 4,\n       \"measure\": \"Net income\",\n       \"period\": \"second quarter of fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$59.68 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 5,\n       \"measure\": \"Net income growth year over year\",\n       \"period\": \"second quarter of fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"126%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 6,\n       \"measure\": \"Earnings per share\",\n       \"period\": \"second quarter of fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$2.46\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 7,\n       \"measure\": \"Earnings per share growth year over year\",\n       \"period\": \"second quarter of fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"128%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 8,\n       \"measure\": \"Operating expenses\",\n       \"period\": \"second quarter of fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$8.4 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 9,\n       \"measure\": \"Gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"second quarter of fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"75%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e6\",\n     \"summary\": \"The consensus revenue estimate for NVIDIA's next earnings report, expected in late November, is $109.0 billion, up 91.2% from the previous year, according to the article.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Nvidia is expected to report another strong quarter when it next reports earnings (likely in late November), with the consensus revenue estimate of $109.0 billion, up 91.2% from the previous year.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"consensus\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Consensus revenue estimate\",\n       \"period\": \"next quarterly report (likely late November)\",\n       \"value\": \"$109.0 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"consensus\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Consensus revenue growth year over year\",\n       \"period\": \"next quarterly report (likely late November)\",\n       \"value\": \"91.2%\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"consensus\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B26\",\n   \"article\": 3,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": \"none kept\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B27\",\n   \"article\": 7,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-1\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management expects Vera Rubin, its newest platform, to be the fastest product ramp in the company's history, and sizes NVIDIA's revenue opportunity at about $40 billion per gigawatt of capacity with Vera Rubin, against $25 billion with Blackwell.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"NVIDIA expects Vera Rubin, its newest platform, to be the fastest product ramp in the company’s history. Management puts NVIDIA’s revenue opportunity at about $40 billion per gigawatt of capacity with Vera Rubin, against $25 billion with Blackwell.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA management\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue opportunity per gigawatt of capacity with Vera Rubin\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"about $40 billion per gigawatt\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"management's sizing, compared with $25 billion per gigawatt with Blackwell\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA management\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-4\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"Management's expectation that Vera Rubin is the fastest ramp in company history and its revenue intensity per gigawatt ($40 billion vs $25 billion for Blackwell) bears on the scale and timing of the Vera Rubin ramp the thesis tracks, though the item gives no shipment date or confirmation of volume customer shipments or first revenue recognition.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-3\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management guided to about 70% revenue growth for fiscal 2028 — the first time it has guided a full year ahead — with the CEO saying demand is much greater than 70% and that supply is what holds the guide there.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"For fiscal 2028, management has guided about 70% revenue growth, the first time it has guided a full year ahead. That guide does not depend on finding more demand. The CEO says demand is much greater than 70%, and that supply is what holds the guide there.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA management (CEO)\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue growth\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"about 70%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"company's first full-year-ahead guide; management says supply, not demand, holds the guide there\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA management (CEO)\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-5\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in frontier AI labs, part of a demand picture the article says leans on NVIDIA's own balance sheet; management concedes some will call this circular financing and says the compute can be redeployed to other customers.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Some of that demand also leans on NVIDIA’s own balance sheet. NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in frontier AI labs. Management concedes some will call this circular financing and says the compute can be redeployed to other customers.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"investment in frontier AI labs\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"nearly $50 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"cumulative amount invested by NVIDIA, as reported by the article\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-1\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"Reports the equity-investment side of the comparison the thesis tracks — nearly $50 billion invested in frontier AI labs — and management's response to 'circular financing' criticism; the item gives no guarantee, credit-support or revenue-backstop amount.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-6\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA guided its gross margin lower, to settle in the 72% to 73% range in fiscal 2028, as memory prices rose more than management expected, partly offset by price increases NVIDIA has already executed.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Gross margin is guided lower. Memory prices have risen more than management expected, and the guide has that margin settling in the 72% to 73% range in fiscal 2028, helped by price increases NVIDIA has already executed.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA management\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"72% to 73%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"guided range; memory prices higher than management expected, helped by price increases already executed (GAAP/non-GAAP basis not stated)\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA management\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-7\",\n     \"summary\": \"The 33 analyst estimates of NVIDIA's fiscal 2028 earnings span $12.62 to $17.49 a share, a wide band for the year on which its forward multiple is priced.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Analysts also disagree on what that growth earns. The 33 estimates of fiscal 2028 earnings run from $12.62 to $17.49 a share, a wide band for the year the forward multiple is priced on.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"33 unnamed analyst estimates (as reported by Trefis)\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"EPS estimates (range across 33 analysts)\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"$12.62 to $17.49 a share\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"range of 33 analyst estimates\",\n       \"party\": \"33 unnamed analyst estimates (as reported by Trefis)\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B28\",\n   \"article\": 8,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"E1\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's fiscal 2026 revenue, which ended in January 2026, reached nearly $215.9 billion, up roughly 65.5% year over year, producing net income of approximately $120.1 billion and a net margin of approximately 55.6%, consistent with the 55.8% net margin in the previous fiscal year, according to Fool.com's comparison article.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Revenue growth remains a primary driver for the business. In fiscal 2026, which ended in January 2026, revenue reached nearly $215.9 billion, up roughly 65.5% year over year. This scale produced net income of approximately $120.1 billion. The net margin remained remarkably high at approximately 55.6%, consistent with the 55.8% net margin achieved in the previous fiscal year.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2026\",\n       \"value\": \"nearly $215.9 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"fiscal year ended in January 2026\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue growth\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2026\",\n       \"value\": \"roughly 65.5%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"year over year\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"Net income\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2026\",\n       \"value\": \"approximately $120.1 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 4,\n       \"measure\": \"Net margin\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2026\",\n       \"value\": \"approximately 55.6%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"versus 55.8% in the previous fiscal year\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"E2\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA reported that sales to two direct customers represented 22% and 14% of total revenue, respectively, in fiscal 2026, according to Fool.com's comparison article.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Nvidia relies on a concentrated customer base, with sales to two direct customers representing 22% and 14% of total revenue in fiscal 2026, respectively.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Share of total revenue from one direct customer\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2026\",\n       \"value\": \"22%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"direct customer\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Share of total revenue from a second direct customer\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2026\",\n       \"value\": \"14%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"direct customer\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"E3\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's data center business generated $89 billion of revenue in its latest quarter alone, with the company's total revenue in that quarter more than doubling year over year and almost all of it coming from data center customers, according to Fool.com's comparison article.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Nvidia's data center business brought in $89 billion in its latest quarter alone, and management guided the next quarter to $108 billion, excluding any China compute sales.\",\n      \"Revenue in its latest quarter also more than doubled, with almost all of it coming from data center customers, and management's outlook for the current quarter assumes no data center compute sales to China at all.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Data center revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"latest quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$89 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"data center business\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"E4\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management guided the next quarter's revenue to $108 billion, an outlook that assumes no data center compute sales to China at all, as reported by Fool.com.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Nvidia's data center business brought in $89 billion in its latest quarter alone, and management guided the next quarter to $108 billion, excluding any China compute sales.\",\n      \"Revenue in its latest quarter also more than doubled, with almost all of it coming from data center customers, and management's outlook for the current quarter assumes no data center compute sales to China at all.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"next quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$108 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"excluding any China compute sales / assumes no data center compute sales to China at all\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA management\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"E5\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA recently agreed to acquire Hugging Face for approximately $12.9 billion to expand its software capabilities, according to Fool.com's comparison article; the item does not say whether the deal has completed.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"The company also recently agreed to acquire Hugging Face for approximately $12.9 billion to expand its software capabilities.\",\n      \"Nvidia has its own concentration risk, with two customers making up more than a third of sales, and the Hugging Face deal shows it's willing to spend to stay ahead.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"recently\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Acquisition consideration\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"approximately $12.9 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"agreed acquisition of Hugging Face; not stated as completed\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ]\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B29\",\n   \"article\": 2,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e1\",\n     \"summary\": \"Quartz, citing Bloomberg-compiled expectations, reports that NVIDIA's revenue and net income are each expected to grow by roughly 90% or more in its current fiscal year, even as the stock trades below 17 times forward earnings.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Nvidia $NVDA stock is trading near its lowest valuation in more than a decade, according to Bloomberg , even as the chipmaker's revenue and net income are each expected to grow by roughly 90% or more in its current fiscal year.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Bloomberg (compiled analyst estimates)\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue growth\",\n       \"period\": \"current fiscal year (FY2027)\",\n       \"value\": \"roughly 90% or more\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"consensus expectation compiled by Bloomberg, per Quartz\",\n       \"party\": \"Bloomberg (compiled analyst estimates)\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"net income growth\",\n       \"period\": \"current fiscal year (FY2027)\",\n       \"value\": \"roughly 90% or more\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"consensus expectation compiled by Bloomberg, per Quartz\",\n       \"party\": \"Bloomberg (compiled analyst estimates)\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e2\",\n     \"summary\": \"At NVIDIA's fiscal second-quarter earnings (reported before 22 September 2026), the company's revenue more than doubled year over year and NVIDIA projected 70% revenue growth for fiscal 2028, well above the 45% analysts had expected at the time.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"The valuation discount follows a stretch of strong results. Nvidia stock climbed roughly 7% after its second-quarter earnings , when revenue more than doubled and the company projected 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028 — well above the 45% analysts had expected at the time.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue growth\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter (FY2027)\",\n       \"value\": \"more than doubled\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"year-over-year, as reported by Quartz\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue growth guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"70%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"company projection made at the fiscal Q2 earnings\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"analyst revenue growth expectation\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"45%\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"analysts' expectation at the time of the Q2 earnings, per Quartz\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e3\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's gross margin came in at 75% in its last reported quarter (fiscal Q2, before 22 September 2026), according to Quartz.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Nvidia 's gross margin came in at 75% last quarter\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"last quarter (fiscal Q2 FY2027)\",\n       \"value\": \"75%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e4\",\n     \"summary\": \"Analyst estimates compiled by Bloomberg, reported by Quartz, show NVIDIA's gross margin sliding from 75% to under 72% by the fourth quarter, with a recovery expected thereafter; rising costs for key components such as memory chips are described as a central factor.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"but analyst estimates compiled by Bloomberg show it sliding under 72% by the fourth quarter, with a recovery expected thereafter.\",\n      \"Rising costs for key components such as memory chips are a central factor.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Bloomberg (compiled analyst estimates)\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"fourth quarter (FY2027)\",\n       \"value\": \"under 72%\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"analyst estimates compiled by Bloomberg\",\n       \"party\": \"Bloomberg (compiled analyst estimates)\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-2\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"Rising costs for key components such as memory chips are the stated driver of the expected margin squeeze; this bears on the input-cost side of the purchase and supply commitments whose disclosed dollar amount clean-2 tracks. It is an input-cost signal only: the item gives no commitment, volume or purchase-obligation figures.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B30\",\n   \"article\": 7,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e1\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management puts the company's revenue opportunity at about $40 billion per gigawatt of computing capacity with its Vera Rubin platform, compared with $25 billion per gigawatt with the prior Blackwell generation.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Management puts NVIDIA’s revenue opportunity at about $40 billion per gigawatt of capacity with Vera Rubin, against $25 billion with Blackwell.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue opportunity per gigawatt of capacity, Vera Rubin\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"about $40 billion per gigawatt of capacity\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"Management's revenue-opportunity framing for the Vera Rubin platform\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue opportunity per gigawatt of capacity, Blackwell\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"$25 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"Management's comparison figure for the Blackwell platform\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-4\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"Quantifies the revenue NVIDIA says each gigawatt of Vera Rubin capacity represents ($40B vs $25B for Blackwell), bearing on how much revenue the Vera Rubin ramp tracked by the thesis could generate; it is management's framing, not a shipment or revenue figure.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e3\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management has guided revenue growth of about 70% for fiscal 2028, the first time the company has guided a full year ahead.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"For fiscal 2028, management has guided about 70% revenue growth, the first time it has guided a full year ahead.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue growth\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"about 70%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"Full-year company revenue growth guidance; first full-year guide the company has given\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e5\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in frontier AI labs; management concedes some will call this circular financing and says the compute can be redeployed to other customers.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Some of that demand also leans on NVIDIA’s own balance sheet.\",\n      \"NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in frontier AI labs.\",\n      \"Management concedes some will call this circular financing and says the compute can be redeployed to other customers.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Invested in frontier AI labs\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"nearly $50 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"Cumulative investment in frontier AI labs, as reported\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-1\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"Gives the scale of NVIDIA's equity investments in frontier AI labs (nearly $50 billion) and management's response to circular-financing criticism - the comparison base against the credit support and revenue guarantees the thesis tracks; no guarantee or backstop amount is stated.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e6\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA guides gross margin lower and expects it to settle in the 72% to 73% range in fiscal 2028, as memory prices rose more than management expected, partly offset by price increases NVIDIA has already implemented.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Gross margin is guided lower.\",\n      \"Memory prices have risen more than management expected, and the guide has that margin settling in the 72% to 73% range in fiscal 2028, helped by price increases NVIDIA has already executed.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"72% to 73%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"Company gross margin guide, set against higher-than-expected memory prices and already-executed price increases\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e7\",\n     \"summary\": \"The 33 analyst estimates of NVIDIA's fiscal 2028 earnings per share range from $12.62 to $17.49 a share, a wide band for the year on which the forward multiple is priced.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"The 33 estimates of fiscal 2028 earnings run from $12.62 to $17.49 a share, a wide band for the year the forward multiple is priced on.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"analysts (33 estimates)\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Earnings per share, range of analyst estimates\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"$12.62 to $17.49 a share\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"Range of 33 individual analyst estimates\",\n       \"party\": \"analysts (33 estimates)\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ]\n  }\n ]\n}","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/figure-fields-2/reader-packet-1-part3.json\\\")); print(json.dumps([{k:a[k] for k in (\\\"article\\\",\\\"title\\\",\\\"publication_date\\\",\\\"text\\\",\\\"named_figures\\\",\\\"open_questions\\\") if k in a} for a in p[\\\"articles\\\"] if a[\\\"article\\\"]>=5],ensure_ascii=False))'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/figure-fields-2/reader-packet-1-part3.json\\\")); print(json.dumps([{k:a[k] for k in (\\\"article\\\",\\\"title\\\",\\\"publication_date\\\",\\\"text\\\",\\\"named_figures\\\",\\\"open_questions\\\") if k in a} for a in p[\\\"articles\\\"] if a[\\\"article\\\"]>=5],ensure_ascii=False))'\"","aggregated_output":"[{\"article\": 5, \"title\": \"Nvidia’s Last Quarter Was Nearly Perfect. One Number in the Fine Print Wasn’t.\", \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-23\", \"text\": \"Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) recently reported $96.2 billion in revenue for the fiscal second quarter, which was 106% more than in the same period last year. The company also produced a 75% gross margin, and management told investors to expect roughly 70% revenue growth in the next fiscal year.\\nBy most numbers, Nvidia reported a stellar quarter. However, a closer look at the company's balance sheet shows that Nvidia's total assets jumped by more than 50% over the past six months. Here's why that matters, and what I'll be watching closely over the next few quarters of Nvidia's AI-driven growth.\\nNvidia's spectacular quarter, with one caveat\\nIt's difficult to overstate how strong Nvidia's fiscal second quarter was. Revenue and earnings both handily beat expectations, and Nvidia guided to a jaw-dropping $108 billion in revenue for the current quarter, representing 17% sequential growth.\\nAs mentioned earlier, Nvidia's total assets grew rapidly. A big part of that is the company's receivables rising from $38.5 billion in January to $63.1 billion at the end of July.\\nOf course, some growth in receivables is to be expected, given Nvidia's spectacular revenue growth. After all, if you sell more products, you can expect more unpaid invoices at any given time. But one number to note is \\\"days sales outstanding,\\\" or DSO. This is the measure of how long, on average, it takes a company to collect after making a sale. Nvidia's DSO went from 45 to 60 days in one quarter. Customers are taking longer to pay.\\nTo be fair, Nvidia told us why. The company extended payment terms on large orders from certain customers.\\nOn one hand, Nvidia offering of flexible terms to its strongest customers helps them lock in multi-year deals. On the other hand, Nvidia is increasingly helping finance its customers' orders, which raises the question of whether demand appears higher in the revenue numbers than it otherwise would.\\nThis is just the latest in a string of \\\"circular\\\" financial moves. For example, Nvidia agreed to guarantee up to $105 billion of data center lease obligations for OpenAI. It has formed partnerships to raise over $500 billion of outside capital for AI infrastructure. In simple terms, Nvidia is actively helping customers pay for its products.\\nThis is especially worth watching for Nvidia stock investors, given the recent push to slow down the pace of AI development. While a call to slow AI development isn't the same thing as a cut to infrastructure budgets, the DSO number, as well as receivables and inventories, are certainly worth keeping an eye on over the next few quarters.\\nIf the DSO number stabilizes, and receivables and inventories rise in line with Nvidia's revenue, it's a very good sign. On the other hand, if these numbers continue to grow faster than the top line, it could indicate a spending slowdown.\", \"named_figures\": [{\"figure\": 1, \"text\": \"$96.2 billion in revenue for the fiscal second quarter\"}, {\"figure\": 2, \"text\": \"roughly 70% revenue growth in the next fiscal year\"}], \"open_questions\": [{\"id\": \"clean-1\", \"question\": \"What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?\", \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-2\", \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?\", \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-3\", \"question\": \"Will Nvidia be permitted to sell its current Blackwell-generation AI chips into China — through U.S. export approval and Chinese acceptance of shipments — by the end of fiscal 2027?\", \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end, used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3 (item gives no date of its own).\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, which the company says has entered full production, reach volume customer shipments (first revenue recognition) by the end of fiscal 2027?\", \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 (FY2027), checked at NVIDIA's quarterly results and FY2027 Form 10-K. The item gives no shipment date; the current fiscal year-end following the September 2026 report that Vera Rubin entered full production is used as the check date.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-5\", \"question\": \"How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2026?\", \"period\": \"Calendar year 2026 (the item's 'this year' in a 23 September 2026 article), checked at the hyperscalers' Q4/full-year 2026 earnings and capex disclosures in early 2027.\"}]}, {\"article\": 6, \"title\": \"I'm Buying More Nvidia Stock Right Now. Here's Why.\", \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-24\", \"text\": \"Nvidia (NASDAQ: NVDA) has grown into the world's largest company, with a market cap of more than $5 trillion. Its graphics processing units (GPUs) are the gold standard for training and running high-level computing and artificial intelligence programs.\\nSome people may, however, look at Nvidia's stock price and think that the shine is coming off Jensen Huang's company. Shares are up 21% so far this year, which is fine, but that's not even close to the 239% gain in 2023, the 171% jump in 2024, or even last year's 39% increase. Meanwhile, stocks like Sandisk , Micron , and Intel are all up more than 200% this year and have become Wall Street darlings.\\nAre Nvidia's best days in the rearview mirror? Absolutely not. While the chipmaker may not replicate its massive 2023 gain, Nvidia remains an incredibly profitable company whose growth story is far from over. In fact, I think Nvidia is going to be putting up some wild numbers in the next two or three years, and that's why I'll be buying more Nvidia shares .\\nNvidia is still growing fast, despite its size\\nWhat stands out in Nvidia's most recent earnings report is that the company is still growing at a triple-digit rate. Revenue in the second quarter of fiscal 2027 (ending July 26, 2026) was $96.2 billion, up 106% from a year ago. Of that, $89 billion, or roughly 92.5% of the company's total revenue, came from its data center segment.\\nNet income was $59.68 billion, up 126% from a year ago, and earnings per share of $2.46 were up 128% from last year. Nvidia is very profitable -- its operating expenses totaled only $8.4 billion for the quarter, and its gross margin was an impressive 75%.\\n\\\"We're the only company in the world that creates and builds, offers an entire AI factory platform, a full-stack system. And customers can still mix and match,\\\" CEO Jensen Huang said. \\\"However, most companies just don't have the skills to do that or desire to do that. And so, there's an entire part of the market that we experience growth. There's sovereign AI, there're regional AIs, there're neoclouds, there're AI start-ups, there're enterprises, where we're seeing -- which represents about half of our business, and that's growing 100% a year.\\\"\\nNvidia's new product line is just getting started\\nNvidia's Hopper and Blackwell architectures have been enormously popular among hyperscalers and neoclouds building computing capacity to meet the growing demand for AI. But the company's Vera Rubin architecture, which uses NVLink chip-to-chip interconnects so its Rubin GPUs and Vera central processing units (CPUs) can share memory and improve efficiency, generates even more revenue. CFO Colette Kress said the Vera Rubin -- now in full production -- and full-stack AI platform has expanded the company's total addressable market from a range of $18 billion to $25 billion per gigawatt from Blackwell to $40 billion from Vera Rubin.\\nThat's one of the reasons why Huang has been so confident in his prediction -- which he made last year and recently reiterated -- that AI infrastructure spending would reach $3 trillion to $4 trillion by 2030. And speaking to reporters last week while in Scotland, Huang suggested that Nvidia would double its chip sales in the coming year.\\nIt's hard to get a group of people to agree on anything -- that's just human nature -- but Wall Street experts are nearly unanimous on Nvidia stock. Analysts at Rosenblatt have a \\\"Buy\\\" recommendation and a $390 price target, representing potential upside of 73%. Bernstein's analysts have a $400 price target and an \\\"Outperform\\\" rating, and Raymond James analysts have a whopping $515 price target and a \\\"Strong Buy\\\" rating.\\nNvidia is expected to report another strong quarter when it next reports earnings (likely in late November), with the consensus revenue estimate of $109.0 billion, up 91.2% from the previous year. And more of the same is expected for next year, following the Vera Rubin ramp-up.\", \"named_figures\": [{\"figure\": 1, \"text\": \"the consensus revenue estimate of $109.0 billion for the next report\"}, {\"figure\": 2, \"text\": \"revenue of $96.2 billion in the second quarter of fiscal 2027\"}], \"open_questions\": [{\"id\": \"clean-1\", \"question\": \"What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?\", \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-2\", \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?\", \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-3\", \"question\": \"Will Nvidia be permitted to sell its current Blackwell-generation AI chips into China — through U.S. export approval and Chinese acceptance of shipments — by the end of fiscal 2027?\", \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end, used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3 (item gives no date of its own).\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, which the company says has entered full production, reach volume customer shipments (first revenue recognition) by the end of fiscal 2027?\", \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 (FY2027), checked at NVIDIA's quarterly results and FY2027 Form 10-K. The item gives no shipment date; the current fiscal year-end following the September 2026 report that Vera Rubin entered full production is used as the check date.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-5\", \"question\": \"How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2026?\", \"period\": \"Calendar year 2026 (the item's 'this year' in a 23 September 2026 article), checked at the hyperscalers' Q4/full-year 2026 earnings and capex disclosures in early 2027.\"}]}, {\"article\": 7, \"title\": \"NVIDIA Stock Looks Expensive Until You Price The Vera Rubin Ramp\", \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-24\", \"text\": \"NVIDIA Stock Looks Expensive Until You Price The Vera Rubin Ramp\\nNVIDIA (NVDA) stock trades near $225, about 28.3 times its adjusted earnings over the past twelve months. Adjusted here means normalized net income with stock-based compensation added back, the same broad basis analysts use in their forecasts. For a stock near its 52-week high, that looks steep. But the multiple leaves out the growth analysts already expect, and that growth depends on how much NVIDIA can supply.\\nNVIDIA Looks Ordinary Once The Forecast Earnings Arrive\\nOn the earnings analysts expect for fiscal 2027, the same price is about 24.1 times. On what they expect for fiscal 2028, it is about 14.5 times. The price is the same. The earnings analysts expect it to buy are not.\\nNVIDIA expects Vera Rubin, its newest platform, to be the fastest product ramp in the company’s history. Management puts NVIDIA’s revenue opportunity at about $40 billion per gigawatt of capacity with Vera Rubin, against $25 billion with Blackwell. Production shipments began in August, with purchase orders already in from every major hyperscaler, AI cloud, and system OEM.\\nSo What Stands Between NVIDIA And Those Earnings?\\nFor fiscal 2028, management has guided about 70% revenue growth, the first time it has guided a full year ahead. That guide does not depend on finding more demand. The CEO says demand is much greater than 70%, and that supply is what holds the guide there. The CFO expects supply to stay a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028 and calls memory pricing extreme.\\nSome of that demand also leans on NVIDIA’s own balance sheet. NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in frontier AI labs. Management concedes some will call this circular financing and says the compute can be redeployed to other customers.\\nAnalysts also disagree on what that growth earns. The 33 estimates of fiscal 2028 earnings run from $12.62 to $17.49 a share, a wide band for the year the forward multiple is priced on.\\nAnd NVIDIA’s Gross Margin Is Guided Lower As Memory Costs Climb\\nConsensus has earnings and revenue growing at a similar pace between fiscal 2027 and fiscal 2028, so analysts assume profit margins hold roughly steady. Gross margin is guided lower. Memory prices have risen more than management expected, and the guide has that margin settling in the 72% to 73% range in fiscal 2028, helped by price increases NVIDIA has already executed.\\nFor the forecast to hold, that lost gross margin has to be made up further down the income statement. That is the second condition behind the fiscal 2028 multiple, alongside supply.\\nSo Is NVIDIA Cheap, Or Only If The Chips Get Built?\\nOnly if the chips get built, which means the supply arrives and the margins hold. The trailing multiple cannot tell you either. How much would you stake on a year that has not started?\\nChecking that for every stock you own is a job most people have no time for. Since its inception, our rule-based Trefis High Quality Portfolio has outperformed its benchmark, a blend of three major indices.\\nOr hunt for these yourself: our Forward Valuation Discount screen ranks stocks priced furthest below their expected earnings. It shows where to look, and the forecast still has to come true.\", \"named_figures\": [{\"figure\": 1, \"text\": \"about 70% revenue growth guided for fiscal 2028\"}, {\"figure\": 2, \"text\": \"the margin settling in the 72% to 73% range in fiscal 2028\"}], \"open_questions\": [{\"id\": \"clean-1\", \"question\": \"What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?\", \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-2\", \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?\", \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-3\", \"question\": \"Will Nvidia be permitted to sell its current Blackwell-generation AI chips into China — through U.S. export approval and Chinese acceptance of shipments — by the end of fiscal 2027?\", \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end, used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3 (item gives no date of its own).\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, which the company says has entered full production, reach volume customer shipments (first revenue recognition) by the end of fiscal 2027?\", \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 (FY2027), checked at NVIDIA's quarterly results and FY2027 Form 10-K. The item gives no shipment date; the current fiscal year-end following the September 2026 report that Vera Rubin entered full production is used as the check date.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-5\", \"question\": \"How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2026?\", \"period\": \"Calendar year 2026 (the item's 'this year' in a 23 September 2026 article), checked at the hyperscalers' Q4/full-year 2026 earnings and capex disclosures in early 2027.\"}]}, {\"article\": 8, \"title\": \"Credo vs. Nvidia: Which AI Chip Stock Is a Better Buy in 2026?\", \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-25\", \"text\": \"The artificial intelligence boom has transformed the semiconductor landscape. Deciding between a high-growth connectivity specialist like Credo Technology Group ( CRDO +5.75% ) and the industry giant Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) requires a look at their specific niches.\\nBoth companies are central to modern data centers, but they solve different problems. Credo provides the high-speed wiring and connectivity chips that keep data moving, while Nvidia supplies the massive processing power AI models require. Comparing these two reveals how different players profit from the same infrastructure trend.\\nCredo designs high-speed copper and optical interconnect solutions essential for modern AI-driven infrastructure. The company operates as a niche provider of high-speed connectivity solutions among semiconductor stocks serving AI data centers. Credo relies on a limited number of customers for a substantial portion of its revenue, with sales to its top 10 customers accounting for approximately 90% of total revenue in fiscal 2026. This level of customer concentration adds risk to the business. The company also maintains a partnership with Oracle ( ORCL -0.13% ) to develop ZeroFlap optics to improve reliability in AI data centers.\\nFinancial performance has accelerated significantly recently. In fiscal 2026, which ended in May 2026, revenue reached nearly $1.3 billion, up roughly 205.7% from the previous year. This growth helped the company achieve a net income of approximately $472.3 million. The net margin for the period was approximately 35.4%, a substantial improvement from the 11.9% net margin reported in the prior fiscal year.\\nThe balance sheet remains highly liquid. As of its May 2026 balance sheet, the current ratio (a measure of short-term debt-paying ability) is nearly 10.2x. The debt-to-equity ratio (total debt compared to equity) is 0.0x, indicating no debt relative to equity. Free cash flow (cash from operations minus capital expenditures) was nearly $407.0 million. Stock-based compensation represented roughly 39.3% of operating cash flow, which inflates reported cash generation because SBC is a non-cash expense added back in the cash flow statement.\\nNvidia is the dominant provider of GPU-accelerated computing platforms used in gaming, professional visualization, and data centers. The company sells its products to massive cloud service providers and AI model makers globally. Nvidia relies on a concentrated customer base, with sales to two direct customers representing 22% and 14% of total revenue in fiscal 2026, respectively. This level of customer concentration adds a layer of risk to the business. The company also recently agreed to acquire Hugging Face for approximately $12.9 billion to expand its software capabilities.\\nRevenue growth remains a primary driver for the business. In fiscal 2026, which ended in January 2026, revenue reached nearly $215.9 billion, up roughly 65.5% year over year. This scale produced net income of approximately $120.1 billion. The net margin remained remarkably high at approximately 55.6%, consistent with the 55.8% net margin achieved in the previous fiscal year.\\nStrong profitability supports a robust capital structure. As of its January 2026 balance sheet, the current ratio is approximately 3.9x. The debt-to-equity ratio (which compares total debt to shareholder equity) is under 0.1x, meaning the company carries very little debt. Free cash flow (cash from operations minus capital expenditures) reached nearly $96.7 billion for the fiscal year. This massive cash generation provides significant flexibility for research, development, and strategic acquisitions.\\nCredo faces intense competition from larger, well-established semiconductor companies and potential new entrants in the data infrastructure market. The company depends heavily on a small number of customers, making it vulnerable to losses or shifts in purchasing patterns. Its business model relies on third-party manufacturing partners, exposing the company to supply chain disruptions and geopolitical risks regarding operations in Taiwan and mainland China. Furthermore, evolving international trade controls and export restrictions could restrict its ability to serve key markets.\\nNvidia faces significant risks from global geopolitical tensions and export control regulations, particularly for high-performance AI chips. These restrictions have impacted sales to China and could continue to limit market access. The company faces intense competition from other hardware suppliers and internal development efforts by customers like Microsoft ( MSFT +1.22% ) , Amazon ( AMZN +0.51% ) , and Alphabet ( GOOGL -0.61% ) ( GOOG -0.77% ) . Nvidia also manages various legal challenges, including inquiries from competition regulators worldwide regarding its market practices.\\nNvidia trades at a lower forward P/E based on future earnings estimates, while both carry a high P/S ratio compared to historical averages.\\nI'd buy Nvidia, and it isn't close. Here's why:\\nCredo's growth is not the issue. Revenue in its latest quarter more than doubled from a year earlier, and management guided the current quarter higher still. The stock fell anyway because gross margin guidance came in flat, and shares now sit well below their 52-week high. That's the problem with a company that gets about 90% of its revenue from 10 customers. Any hint of slower spending from one of them moves the stock, and the valuation leaves no room for a miss.\\nNvidia is growing nearly as fast at a scale that makes Credo look like a rounding error. Revenue in its latest quarter also more than doubled, with almost all of it coming from data center customers, and management's outlook for the current quarter assumes no data center compute sales to China at all. Nvidia has its own concentration risk, with two customers making up more than a third of sales, and the Hugging Face deal shows it's willing to spend to stay ahead.\\nFor a long-term investor, Nvidia offers the same AI infrastructure exposure at a lower price per dollar of expected earnings, with far less dependence on any single buyer. Credo belongs in a portfolio only as a small position you can afford to watch swing.\\nCredo more than tripled revenue last year but leans on 10 customers for 90% of sales, while Nvidia is doubling revenue at a scale nobody else in AI hardware can match.\\nBy Mike Schwenk – Updated Sep 25, 2026 at 3:00PM EST\\nCredo makes the high-speed connectivity chips and cables that move data between servers inside AI data centers, and revenue more than tripled in fiscal 2026.\\nNvidia's data center business brought in $89 billion in its latest quarter alone, and management guided the next quarter to $108 billion, excluding any China compute sales.\\nBoth companies depend on a small group of customers, but Nvidia's scale, margins, and lower valuation make it the stronger buy today.\", \"named_figures\": [], \"open_questions\": null}]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/figure-fields-2/reader-packet-1-part3.json\\\"));\nfor a in p[\\\"answers\\\"]:\n print(\\\"ANSWER\\\",a[\\\"answer\\\"],\\\"ARTICLE\\\",a[\\\"article\\\"])\n for s in a[\\\"statements_with_figures\\\"]:\n  print(json.dumps({k:s.get(k) for k in (\\\"id\\\",\\\"summary\\\",\\\"statement_party\\\",\\\"statement_date\\\",\\\"figures\\\",\\\"links\\\") if k in s},ensure_ascii=False))'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/figure-fields-2/reader-packet-1-part3.json\\\"));\nfor a in p[\\\"answers\\\"]:\n print(\\\"ANSWER\\\",a[\\\"answer\\\"],\\\"ARTICLE\\\",a[\\\"article\\\"])\n for s in a[\\\"statements_with_figures\\\"]:\n  print(json.dumps({k:s.get(k) for k in (\\\"id\\\",\\\"summary\\\",\\\"statement_party\\\",\\\"statement_date\\\",\\\"figures\\\",\\\"links\\\") if k in s},ensure_ascii=False))'\"","aggregated_output":"ANSWER B21 ARTICLE 1\n{\"id\": \"ev-1\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's latest reported quarter generated $89 billion of data-center revenue out of $96.2 billion in total revenue, with GuruFocus calculating that roughly 92.5% of sales came from the infrastructure businesses powering large-scale AI. The item does not specify which fiscal quarter this is.\", \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"data-center revenue\", \"period\": \"latest quarter\", \"value\": \"$89 billion\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"data-center / AI infrastructure businesses\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}, {\"n\": 2, \"measure\": \"total revenue\", \"period\": \"latest quarter\", \"value\": \"$96.2 billion\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": null, \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}\nANSWER B22 ARTICLE 5\n{\"id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"Nvidia reported $96.2 billion of revenue for its fiscal second quarter, 106% more than in the same period a year earlier, at a 75% gross margin; the article says revenue and earnings handily beat expectations.\", \"statement_party\": \"Nvidia\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"revenue\", \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter\", \"value\": \"$96.2 billion\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": null, \"party\": \"Nvidia\"}, {\"n\": 2, \"measure\": \"revenue growth\", \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter versus same period last year\", \"value\": \"106%\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"growth over the same period last year\", \"party\": \"Nvidia\"}, {\"n\": 3, \"measure\": \"gross margin\", \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter\", \"value\": \"75%\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": null, \"party\": \"Nvidia\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}\n{\"id\": \"e2\", \"summary\": \"Nvidia guided investors to $108 billion of revenue for the current quarter, which it said represents 17% sequential growth.\", \"statement_party\": \"Nvidia\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"revenue guidance\", \"period\": \"current quarter\", \"value\": \"$108 billion\", \"kind\": \"guidance\", \"basis\": null, \"party\": \"Nvidia\"}, {\"n\": 2, \"measure\": \"sequential revenue growth\", \"period\": \"current quarter\", \"value\": \"17%\", \"kind\": \"guidance\", \"basis\": \"sequential growth\", \"party\": \"Nvidia\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}\n{\"id\": \"e3\", \"summary\": \"Nvidia management told investors to expect roughly 70% revenue growth in the next fiscal year.\", \"statement_party\": \"Nvidia\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"revenue growth guidance\", \"period\": \"next fiscal year\", \"value\": \"70%\", \"kind\": \"guidance\", \"basis\": \"roughly\", \"party\": \"Nvidia\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}\n{\"id\": \"e5\", \"summary\": \"Nvidia's receivables rose from $38.5 billion in January to $63.1 billion at the end of July, as total assets jumped by more than 50% over the past six months, and days sales outstanding rose from 45 to 60 days in one quarter; Nvidia said it extended payment terms on large orders from certain customers.\", \"statement_party\": \"Nvidia\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"accounts receivable\", \"period\": \"January\", \"value\": \"$38.5 billion\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": null, \"party\": \"Nvidia\"}, {\"n\": 2, \"measure\": \"accounts receivable\", \"period\": \"end of July\", \"value\": \"$63.1 billion\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": null, \"party\": \"Nvidia\"}, {\"n\": 3, \"measure\": \"days sales outstanding\", \"period\": \"one quarter\", \"value\": \"60 days\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"up from 45 days\", \"party\": \"Nvidia\"}], \"links\": [{\"question\": \"clean-1\", \"new_question\": false, \"reason\": \"Shows NVIDIA financing customers' purchases through extended payment terms on large orders, alongside the formal guarantees the thesis tracks; the receivables jump and DSO rise of 45 to 60 days indicate the scale of that customer credit exposure, but the item gives no guarantee amounts.\"}]}\nANSWER B23 ARTICLE 4\n{\"id\": \"ev-1\", \"summary\": \"Motley Fool contributor Jeremy Bowman reported that Wall Street expects Nvidia to grow revenue by 91% this year and 66% next year, and that Nvidia is expected to earn $15.68 per share in fiscal 2028, which the article says ends in January of that year. These are third-party/consensus forecasts, not company guidance; the article gives no period year for the two revenue growth rates beyond 'this year' and 'next year'.\", \"statement_party\": \"consensus\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"revenue growth\", \"period\": \"this year\", \"value\": \"91%\", \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"company revenue growth, attributed to Wall Street expectations\", \"party\": \"consensus\"}, {\"n\": 2, \"measure\": \"revenue growth\", \"period\": \"next year\", \"value\": \"66%\", \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"company revenue growth, attributed to Wall Street expectations\", \"party\": \"consensus\"}, {\"n\": 3, \"measure\": \"earnings per share\", \"period\": \"fiscal 2028, which ends in January of that year\", \"value\": \"$15.68\", \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"expected EPS, source not named beyond 'expected'\", \"party\": \"consensus\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}\nANSWER B24 ARTICLE 2\n{\"id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"Nvidia's revenue and net income are each expected to grow by roughly 90% or more in its current fiscal year, according to estimates reported by Bloomberg, even as the stock trades near its lowest valuation in more than a decade.\", \"statement_party\": \"Bloomberg (compiled estimates)\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"Revenue growth\", \"period\": \"Current fiscal year\", \"value\": \"roughly 90% or more\", \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"Company-level revenue growth expected, per Bloomberg\", \"party\": \"Bloomberg (compiled estimates)\"}, {\"n\": 2, \"measure\": \"Net income growth\", \"period\": \"Current fiscal year\", \"value\": \"roughly 90% or more\", \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"Company-level net income growth expected, per Bloomberg\", \"party\": \"Bloomberg (compiled estimates)\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}\n{\"id\": \"e2\", \"summary\": \"Nvidia's gross margin came in at 75% in the last reported quarter, and analyst estimates compiled by Bloomberg show it sliding under 72% by the fourth quarter, with a recovery expected thereafter; rising costs for key components such as memory chips are cited as a central factor.\", \"statement_party\": \"Nvidia (reported gross margin); Bloomberg-compiled analyst estimates\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"Gross margin\", \"period\": \"Last quarter\", \"value\": \"75%\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"Company-level gross margin, as reported\", \"party\": \"Nvidia (reported gross margin); Bloomberg-compiled analyst estimates\"}, {\"n\": 2, \"measure\": \"Gross margin\", \"period\": \"Fourth quarter\", \"value\": \"under 72%\", \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"Analyst estimates compiled by Bloomberg\", \"party\": \"Nvidia (reported gross margin); Bloomberg-compiled analyst estimates\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}\n{\"id\": \"e3\", \"summary\": \"Nvidia reported second-quarter revenue that more than doubled year over year and projected 70% revenue growth for fiscal 2028, well above the 45% analysts had expected at the time; the stock rose roughly 7% after the earnings report.\", \"statement_party\": \"Nvidia (results and guidance); analysts (prior expectation)\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"Revenue growth\", \"period\": \"Second quarter\", \"value\": \"more than doubled\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"Year-over-year company revenue growth, as reported\", \"party\": \"Nvidia (results and guidance); analysts (prior expectation)\"}, {\"n\": 2, \"measure\": \"Revenue growth guidance\", \"period\": \"Fiscal 2028\", \"value\": \"70%\", \"kind\": \"guidance\", \"basis\": \"Company projection, per the article\", \"party\": \"Nvidia (results and guidance); analysts (prior expectation)\"}, {\"n\": 3, \"measure\": \"Revenue growth expectation\", \"period\": \"Fiscal 2028\", \"value\": \"45%\", \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"What analysts had expected at the time, per the article\", \"party\": \"Nvidia (results and guidance); analysts (prior expectation)\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}\nANSWER B25 ARTICLE 6\n{\"id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA reported second-quarter fiscal 2027 (quarter ended 26 July 2026) revenue of $96.2 billion, up 106% from a year earlier, of which $89 billion — roughly 92.5% of total revenue — came from the data center segment. Net income was $59.68 billion, up 126%, and earnings per share were $2.46, up 128%. Operating expenses totaled $8.4 billion for the quarter and gross margin was 75%.\", \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"Revenue\", \"period\": \"second quarter of fiscal 2027\", \"value\": \"$96.2 billion\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": null, \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}, {\"n\": 2, \"measure\": \"Revenue growth year over year\", \"period\": \"second quarter of fiscal 2027\", \"value\": \"106%\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": null, \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}, {\"n\": 3, \"measure\": \"Data center segment revenue\", \"period\": \"second quarter of fiscal 2027\", \"value\": \"$89 billion\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"data center segment; roughly 92.5% of total revenue\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}, {\"n\": 4, \"measure\": \"Net income\", \"period\": \"second quarter of fiscal 2027\", \"value\": \"$59.68 billion\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": null, \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}, {\"n\": 5, \"measure\": \"Net income growth year over year\", \"period\": \"second quarter of fiscal 2027\", \"value\": \"126%\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": null, \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}, {\"n\": 6, \"measure\": \"Earnings per share\", \"period\": \"second quarter of fiscal 2027\", \"value\": \"$2.46\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": null, \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}, {\"n\": 7, \"measure\": \"Earnings per share growth year over year\", \"period\": \"second quarter of fiscal 2027\", \"value\": \"128%\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": null, \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}, {\"n\": 8, \"measure\": \"Operating expenses\", \"period\": \"second quarter of fiscal 2027\", \"value\": \"$8.4 billion\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": null, \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}, {\"n\": 9, \"measure\": \"Gross margin\", \"period\": \"second quarter of fiscal 2027\", \"value\": \"75%\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": null, \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}\n{\"id\": \"e6\", \"summary\": \"The consensus revenue estimate for NVIDIA's next earnings report, expected in late November, is $109.0 billion, up 91.2% from the previous year, according to the article.\", \"statement_party\": \"consensus\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"Consensus revenue estimate\", \"period\": \"next quarterly report (likely late November)\", \"value\": \"$109.0 billion\", \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": null, \"party\": \"consensus\"}, {\"n\": 2, \"measure\": \"Consensus revenue growth year over year\", \"period\": \"next quarterly report (likely late November)\", \"value\": \"91.2%\", \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": null, \"party\": \"consensus\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}\nANSWER B26 ARTICLE 3\n{}\n{}\n{}\n{}\n{}\n{}\n{}\n{}\n{}\nANSWER B27 ARTICLE 7\n{\"id\": \"ev-1\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management expects Vera Rubin, its newest platform, to be the fastest product ramp in the company's history, and sizes NVIDIA's revenue opportunity at about $40 billion per gigawatt of capacity with Vera Rubin, against $25 billion with Blackwell.\", \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA management\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"revenue opportunity per gigawatt of capacity with Vera Rubin\", \"period\": null, \"value\": \"about $40 billion per gigawatt\", \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"management's sizing, compared with $25 billion per gigawatt with Blackwell\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA management\"}], \"links\": [{\"question\": \"clean-4\", \"new_question\": false, \"reason\": \"Management's expectation that Vera Rubin is the fastest ramp in company history and its revenue intensity per gigawatt ($40 billion vs $25 billion for Blackwell) bears on the scale and timing of the Vera Rubin ramp the thesis tracks, though the item gives no shipment date or confirmation of volume customer shipments or first revenue recognition.\"}]}\n{\"id\": \"ev-3\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management guided to about 70% revenue growth for fiscal 2028 — the first time it has guided a full year ahead — with the CEO saying demand is much greater than 70% and that supply is what holds the guide there.\", \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA management (CEO)\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"revenue growth\", \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\", \"value\": \"about 70%\", \"kind\": \"guidance\", \"basis\": \"company's first full-year-ahead guide; management says supply, not demand, holds the guide there\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA management (CEO)\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}\n{\"id\": \"ev-5\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in frontier AI labs, part of a demand picture the article says leans on NVIDIA's own balance sheet; management concedes some will call this circular financing and says the compute can be redeployed to other customers.\", \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"investment in frontier AI labs\", \"period\": null, \"value\": \"nearly $50 billion\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"cumulative amount invested by NVIDIA, as reported by the article\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}], \"links\": [{\"question\": \"clean-1\", \"new_question\": false, \"reason\": \"Reports the equity-investment side of the comparison the thesis tracks — nearly $50 billion invested in frontier AI labs — and management's response to 'circular financing' criticism; the item gives no guarantee, credit-support or revenue-backstop amount.\"}]}\n{\"id\": \"ev-6\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA guided its gross margin lower, to settle in the 72% to 73% range in fiscal 2028, as memory prices rose more than management expected, partly offset by price increases NVIDIA has already executed.\", \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA management\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"gross margin\", \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\", \"value\": \"72% to 73%\", \"kind\": \"guidance\", \"basis\": \"guided range; memory prices higher than management expected, helped by price increases already executed (GAAP/non-GAAP basis not stated)\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA management\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}\n{\"id\": \"ev-7\", \"summary\": \"The 33 analyst estimates of NVIDIA's fiscal 2028 earnings span $12.62 to $17.49 a share, a wide band for the year on which its forward multiple is priced.\", \"statement_party\": \"33 unnamed analyst estimates (as reported by Trefis)\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"EPS estimates (range across 33 analysts)\", \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\", \"value\": \"$12.62 to $17.49 a share\", \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"range of 33 analyst estimates\", \"party\": \"33 unnamed analyst estimates (as reported by Trefis)\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}\nANSWER B28 ARTICLE 8\n{\"id\": \"E1\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's fiscal 2026 revenue, which ended in January 2026, reached nearly $215.9 billion, up roughly 65.5% year over year, producing net income of approximately $120.1 billion and a net margin of approximately 55.6%, consistent with the 55.8% net margin in the previous fiscal year, according to Fool.com's comparison article.\", \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"Revenue\", \"period\": \"fiscal 2026\", \"value\": \"nearly $215.9 billion\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"fiscal year ended in January 2026\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}, {\"n\": 2, \"measure\": \"Revenue growth\", \"period\": \"fiscal 2026\", \"value\": \"roughly 65.5%\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"year over year\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}, {\"n\": 3, \"measure\": \"Net income\", \"period\": \"fiscal 2026\", \"value\": \"approximately $120.1 billion\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": null, \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}, {\"n\": 4, \"measure\": \"Net margin\", \"period\": \"fiscal 2026\", \"value\": \"approximately 55.6%\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"versus 55.8% in the previous fiscal year\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}]}\n{\"id\": \"E2\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA reported that sales to two direct customers represented 22% and 14% of total revenue, respectively, in fiscal 2026, according to Fool.com's comparison article.\", \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"Share of total revenue from one direct customer\", \"period\": \"fiscal 2026\", \"value\": \"22%\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"direct customer\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}, {\"n\": 2, \"measure\": \"Share of total revenue from a second direct customer\", \"period\": \"fiscal 2026\", \"value\": \"14%\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"direct customer\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}]}\n{\"id\": \"E3\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's data center business generated $89 billion of revenue in its latest quarter alone, with the company's total revenue in that quarter more than doubling year over year and almost all of it coming from data center customers, according to Fool.com's comparison article.\", \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"Data center revenue\", \"period\": \"latest quarter\", \"value\": \"$89 billion\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"data center business\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}]}\n{\"id\": \"E4\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management guided the next quarter's revenue to $108 billion, an outlook that assumes no data center compute sales to China at all, as reported by Fool.com.\", \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"Revenue guidance\", \"period\": \"next quarter\", \"value\": \"$108 billion\", \"kind\": \"guidance\", \"basis\": \"excluding any China compute sales / assumes no data center compute sales to China at all\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA management\"}]}\n{\"id\": \"E5\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA recently agreed to acquire Hugging Face for approximately $12.9 billion to expand its software capabilities, according to Fool.com's comparison article; the item does not say whether the deal has completed.\", \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"statement_date\": \"recently\", \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"Acquisition consideration\", \"period\": null, \"value\": \"approximately $12.9 billion\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"agreed acquisition of Hugging Face; not stated as completed\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}]}\nANSWER B29 ARTICLE 2\n{\"id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"Quartz, citing Bloomberg-compiled expectations, reports that NVIDIA's revenue and net income are each expected to grow by roughly 90% or more in its current fiscal year, even as the stock trades below 17 times forward earnings.\", \"statement_party\": \"Bloomberg (compiled analyst estimates)\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"revenue growth\", \"period\": \"current fiscal year (FY2027)\", \"value\": \"roughly 90% or more\", \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"consensus expectation compiled by Bloomberg, per Quartz\", \"party\": \"Bloomberg (compiled analyst estimates)\"}, {\"n\": 2, \"measure\": \"net income growth\", \"period\": \"current fiscal year (FY2027)\", \"value\": \"roughly 90% or more\", \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"consensus expectation compiled by Bloomberg, per Quartz\", \"party\": \"Bloomberg (compiled analyst estimates)\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}\n{\"id\": \"e2\", \"summary\": \"At NVIDIA's fiscal second-quarter earnings (reported before 22 September 2026), the company's revenue more than doubled year over year and NVIDIA projected 70% revenue growth for fiscal 2028, well above the 45% analysts had expected at the time.\", \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"revenue growth\", \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter (FY2027)\", \"value\": \"more than doubled\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"year-over-year, as reported by Quartz\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}, {\"n\": 2, \"measure\": \"revenue growth guidance\", \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\", \"value\": \"70%\", \"kind\": \"guidance\", \"basis\": \"company projection made at the fiscal Q2 earnings\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}, {\"n\": 3, \"measure\": \"analyst revenue growth expectation\", \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\", \"value\": \"45%\", \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"analysts' expectation at the time of the Q2 earnings, per Quartz\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}\n{\"id\": \"e3\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's gross margin came in at 75% in its last reported quarter (fiscal Q2, before 22 September 2026), according to Quartz.\", \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"gross margin\", \"period\": \"last quarter (fiscal Q2 FY2027)\", \"value\": \"75%\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": null, \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}\n{\"id\": \"e4\", \"summary\": \"Analyst estimates compiled by Bloomberg, reported by Quartz, show NVIDIA's gross margin sliding from 75% to under 72% by the fourth quarter, with a recovery expected thereafter; rising costs for key components such as memory chips are described as a central factor.\", \"statement_party\": \"Bloomberg (compiled analyst estimates)\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"gross margin\", \"period\": \"fourth quarter (FY2027)\", \"value\": \"under 72%\", \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"analyst estimates compiled by Bloomberg\", \"party\": \"Bloomberg (compiled analyst estimates)\"}], \"links\": [{\"question\": \"clean-2\", \"new_question\": false, \"reason\": \"Rising costs for key components such as memory chips are the stated driver of the expected margin squeeze; this bears on the input-cost side of the purchase and supply commitments whose disclosed dollar amount clean-2 tracks. It is an input-cost signal only: the item gives no commitment, volume or purchase-obligation figures.\"}]}\nANSWER B30 ARTICLE 7\n{\"id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management puts the company's revenue opportunity at about $40 billion per gigawatt of computing capacity with its Vera Rubin platform, compared with $25 billion per gigawatt with the prior Blackwell generation.\", \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"Revenue opportunity per gigawatt of capacity, Vera Rubin\", \"period\": null, \"value\": \"about $40 billion per gigawatt of capacity\", \"kind\": \"guidance\", \"basis\": \"Management's revenue-opportunity framing for the Vera Rubin platform\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}, {\"n\": 2, \"measure\": \"Revenue opportunity per gigawatt of capacity, Blackwell\", \"period\": null, \"value\": \"$25 billion\", \"kind\": \"guidance\", \"basis\": \"Management's comparison figure for the Blackwell platform\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}], \"links\": [{\"question\": \"clean-4\", \"new_question\": false, \"reason\": \"Quantifies the revenue NVIDIA says each gigawatt of Vera Rubin capacity represents ($40B vs $25B for Blackwell), bearing on how much revenue the Vera Rubin ramp tracked by the thesis could generate; it is management's framing, not a shipment or revenue figure.\"}]}\n{\"id\": \"e3\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management has guided revenue growth of about 70% for fiscal 2028, the first time the company has guided a full year ahead.\", \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"Revenue growth\", \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\", \"value\": \"about 70%\", \"kind\": \"guidance\", \"basis\": \"Full-year company revenue growth guidance; first full-year guide the company has given\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}\n{\"id\": \"e5\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in frontier AI labs; management concedes some will call this circular financing and says the compute can be redeployed to other customers.\", \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"Invested in frontier AI labs\", \"period\": null, \"value\": \"nearly $50 billion\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"Cumulative investment in frontier AI labs, as reported\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}], \"links\": [{\"question\": \"clean-1\", \"new_question\": false, \"reason\": \"Gives the scale of NVIDIA's equity investments in frontier AI labs (nearly $50 billion) and management's response to circular-financing criticism - the comparison base against the credit support and revenue guarantees the thesis tracks; no guarantee or backstop amount is stated.\"}]}\n{\"id\": \"e6\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA guides gross margin lower and expects it to settle in the 72% to 73% range in fiscal 2028, as memory prices rose more than management expected, partly offset by price increases NVIDIA has already implemented.\", \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"Gross margin\", \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\", \"value\": \"72% to 73%\", \"kind\": \"guidance\", \"basis\": \"Company gross margin guide, set against higher-than-expected memory prices and already-executed price increases\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}\n{\"id\": \"e7\", \"summary\": \"The 33 analyst estimates of NVIDIA's fiscal 2028 earnings per share range from $12.62 to $17.49 a share, a wide band for the year on which the forward multiple is priced.\", \"statement_party\": \"analysts (33 estimates)\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"Earnings per share, range of analyst estimates\", \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\", \"value\": \"$12.62 to $17.49 a share\", \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"Range of 33 individual analyst estimates\", \"party\": \"analysts (33 estimates)\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
